    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The full annual cost of keeping a home treadmill healthy is somewhere between a takeaway
          meal and a tank of fuel. The cost of not doing it is a motor or controller board, and
          those are several hundred dollars each. Very few purchases have that payoff ratio.
        </p>

        <div id="annual-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Annual Figure</h2>
<p>
            For a home treadmill used a few times a week, routine maintenance costs roughly
            <strong>$15 to $40 a year</strong> and takes under an hour in total. That breaks down
            into a very short list.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Silicone lubricant: $10-$18 per bottle</strong>, lasting three to six
            applications. At the standard 40 to 50 hour interval that is one to two years of
            coverage, so $5 to $15 a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cleaning supplies: $8-$15</strong> for isopropyl alcohol and lint-free cloths,
            which last considerably longer than a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A treadmill mat: $40-$90</strong>, bought once. Not strictly maintenance, but
            it keeps dust and carpet fibre out of the motor and stops the frame walking, so it
            belongs in the picture.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is the whole routine cost. There is no annual service requirement on a home
            treadmill, no filter to replace and no fluid to change. The entire discipline is
            lubricate, clean, and check tension and tracking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="time-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Time Cost, Which Is the Real Barrier</h2>
<p>
            Money is not why people skip maintenance — $20 a year deters nobody. Time and
            uncertainty are, and both are smaller than they appear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lubrication: about 10 minutes</strong>, three to four times a year for a
            typically-used machine. Loosen the belt, clean, apply, retension, walk it in.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wiping down after sessions: under a minute</strong>, and it is the cheapest
            thing on this list. Sweat is corrosive and dust is abrasive.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Monthly vacuuming around and under the machine: 5 minutes.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Quarterly tension and tracking check: 5 minutes</strong>, and often no
            adjustment is needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Totalled across a year that is roughly 50 to 70 minutes. Set against a machine lifespan
            that roughly doubles, it is the best-value hour anyone spends on a treadmill. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            lays out the full year, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">printable checklist</a>
            turns it into something you can work through.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wear-parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Wear Parts and What They Cost</h2>
<p>
            Beyond consumables, three components wear out rather than fail, and their replacement
            is a normal part of long ownership rather than a fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running belt: $80-$250 for the part.</strong> Typically 5 to 10 years on a
            maintained machine, considerably less on a neglected one. Fitting is a moderately
            awkward DIY job or $100 to $200 of labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck: $100-$300.</strong> Many decks are reversible — the underside is an
            unused surface — so flipping one costs only labour and effectively doubles deck life.
            Worth checking before buying a replacement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rollers: $60-$200 each.</strong> These rarely need replacing on a home machine
            and announce themselves clearly when they do, as a rumble that rises with speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Belt and deck are frequently replaced together, since the labour overlaps almost
            entirely and a worn belt on a fresh deck wears it prematurely. Budget $250 to $500 all
            in for that job done properly at around year seven or eight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What the Expensive Failures Cost</h2>
<p>
            These are the failures that end treadmills, and they are almost always downstream of
            friction that was left unaddressed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Drive motor: $200-$600 for the part</strong>, plus $100-$250 labour. This is
            the classic end-of-life failure and it is overwhelmingly caused by years of running
            against a dry deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor controller board: $150-$450</strong>, plus labour. Often fails alongside
            or because of the motor, for the same reason — sustained elevated current draw.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline motor: $100-$300.</strong> An awkward but self-contained failure,
            unrelated to belt friction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console or display: $150-$700</strong>, and this is where screen-based machines
            become expensive. A large touchscreen assembly can cost a substantial fraction of a
            mid-range treadmill's value, which is a quiet argument for simpler consoles.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Service call-out: $80-$150</strong> before any parts or labour, and many
            engineers charge it whether or not you proceed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-tier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Cost Varies by Machine Tier</h2>
<p>
            Routine maintenance costs almost the same across the range — a $500 machine and a
            $4,000 machine both take about an ounce of silicone. Repairs do not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Budget machines ($300-$800).</strong> Parts are cheap but frequently
            unavailable, and third-party support is thin. A motor failure at $250 plus labour on a
            $500 machine is close to a total loss, so these are effectively disposable at the point
            of a major fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mid-range ($900-$2,000).</strong> The sweet spot for repairability. Parts are
            available, generally standardised, and worth fitting — a $400 motor repair on a $1,500
            machine that then runs another five years is straightforward value.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Premium and commercial ($2,500+).</strong> Parts cost more but are supported
            for far longer, service networks exist, and the machines are designed to be repaired
            rather than replaced. A $700 repair on a $4,000 machine at year twelve is entirely
            rational.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is one of the better arguments for the mid-range and above: not that the machines
            fail less, but that when they do, fixing them makes sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. When Repair Stops Making Sense</h2>
<p>
            A workable rule: if the total repair cost exceeds half the machine's current
            replacement value, and it is past the midpoint of its expected life, replace it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Worked through: a $400 repair on a four-year-old $1,500 machine is worth doing — the
            machine has years left and $400 buys them. The same $400 repair on a nine-year-old
            $700 machine is not; you are spending most of a new budget machine's price on hardware
            near the end of its life.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three things shift the calculation toward repair. If the failure is a single
            self-contained component like an incline motor, the rest of the machine is unaffected.
            If the deck and belt were recently replaced, the expensive wear items are fresh. And if
            the machine is a commercial-grade unit, its remaining life is much longer than its age
            suggests.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two shift it toward replacement: a second major failure within a year or two, which
            usually indicates the whole drivetrain is at end of life, and a machine that was never
            maintained, where fixing one symptom leaves the underlying friction problem in place.
            Our <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan guide</a>
            covers the full arithmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-vs-professional" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Do Yourself and What Not To</h2>
<p>
            The split is reasonably clear, and it is drawn around the motor compartment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do yourself:</strong> lubrication, cleaning, belt tension adjustment, belt
            tracking and centring, vacuuming around and under the machine, and tightening frame
            fasteners. All are documented, none requires special tools, and all are done with the
            machine unplugged.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reasonable with care:</strong> belt replacement and deck flipping. Both are
            mechanically simple and physically awkward, and both are within reach of anyone
            comfortable with basic tools and a second pair of hands.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Leave to an engineer:</strong> anything involving the motor, the controller
            board or mains wiring. These carry electrical risk, boards can hold charge after
            disconnection, and a misdiagnosis here is expensive. If your diagnosis points inside
            the motor compartment, that is the point to call someone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            covers diagnosing faults safely, including the four symptoms that mean stop using the
            machine entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ten-year-picture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. The Ten-Year Picture</h2>
<p>
            Totalling a decade of ownership on a maintained mid-range machine:
          </p>
          <p>
            Routine maintenance at $20 a year is <strong>$200</strong>. A belt and deck replacement
            around year seven is <strong>$250-$500</strong>. Electricity, at typical home use, is
            roughly <strong>$5-$15 a year</strong> or $50-$150 across the decade — our
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">electricity guide</a>
            works through the figures. One mat at <strong>$60</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            That totals roughly <strong>$560 to $910 over ten years</strong>, or $56 to $91 a year,
            on top of the purchase price. For a $1,500 machine that is a total cost of ownership
            around $2,100 to $2,400 across a decade — comfortably under $250 a year, and against
            three sessions a week it is close to $1.50 a session.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same machine neglected fails around year five with a motor and board failure,
            gets replaced rather than repaired, and costs $3,000 across the same decade for less
            use. The difference is that hour a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            Note what is absent from this list: any subscription. At around $39 a month that alone
            is $4,680 over ten years, dwarfing every maintenance and repair figure on this page.
            Our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">subscription guide</a>
            covers it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What the Warranty Does and Does Not Cover</h2>
<p>
            Warranty terms change the cost picture significantly in the early years, and they are
            worth reading properly rather than by headline.
          </p>
          <p>
            Frame warranties of ten years or lifetime cover the component least likely to fail.
            Parts terms of one to five years cover the components that do. Labour terms — often
            just one year — are the ones that matter most, because labour is a large share of any
            repair and shipping a 300 lb machine anywhere is not a realistic remedy.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two clauses are worth finding. Whether service is in-home or return-to-base, since
            return-to-base on a treadmill is close to no cover at all. And whether the warranty
            requires documented maintenance — some manufacturers can decline a motor claim on a
            deck that was clearly never lubricated, which turns skipped maintenance into a direct
            financial loss rather than an indirect one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extended-warranties" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Are Extended Warranties Worth It?</h2>
<p>
            Retailers push extended cover hard on treadmills, and the honest answer is that it
            depends almost entirely on the machine's price tier and on what the policy actually
            covers.
          </p>
          <p>
            On a <strong>budget machine</strong>, generally no. A three-year extension costing
            $150 on a $600 treadmill is a quarter of the purchase price to insure a machine whose
            major failure would be a write-off anyway. You are better off putting that money
            toward a better machine in the first place.
          </p>
          <p>
            On a <strong>mid-range machine</strong>, it is genuinely arguable. A motor and
            controller failure at year four costs $400 to $800 all in, which is what these
            policies are for, and mid-range machines are worth repairing. The question is the
            exclusions rather than the price.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read for three things specifically. Whether cover is in-home or return-to-base, since
            shipping a 300 lb machine is not a real remedy. Whether wear items — belt, deck,
            rollers — are excluded, because they usually are, and they are the parts most likely
            to need attention. And whether a maintenance record is required, since a policy that
            can be declined over an unlubricated deck is worth considerably less than it appears.
          </p>
          <p>
            The general pattern is that extended cover is priced to be profitable for the seller,
            and the reliable way to beat it is the routine at the top of this page. Money spent on
            lubricant addresses the same failures at roughly one per cent of the cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            Budget <strong>$20 to $40 a year</strong> for routine maintenance and about an hour of
            your time. That is the whole ongoing cost, and it roughly doubles the machine's life.
          </p>
          <p>
            Set aside a further <strong>$250 to $500 around year seven</strong> for a belt and deck
            replacement, which is normal wear rather than failure. Anything beyond that is
            avoidable in most cases, because the expensive failures are downstream of friction.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a major fault does arrive, apply the rule: repair if the cost is under half the
            machine's replacement value and it is not past the midpoint of its life. Otherwise
            replace, and maintain the next one.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you take one number from this page, take this: $20 a year against $300 to $800
            per avoided failure. There is no other decision in treadmill ownership with that
            payoff.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does treadmill maintenance cost per year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly $15 to $40 a year for a home machine used a few times a week — mostly silicone lubricant at $10 to $18 a bottle lasting one to two years, plus cleaning supplies. There is no annual service requirement, no filter and no fluid change. The time cost is about an hour a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to repair a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A drive motor is $200 to $600 for the part plus $100 to $250 labour. A controller board is $150 to $450 plus labour. An incline motor is $100 to $300, and a console or touchscreen $150 to $700. Most engineers also charge an $80 to $150 call-out before any work begins.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing an old treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the total repair is under half the machine's current replacement value and it is not past the midpoint of its expected life, usually yes. A $400 repair on a four-year-old $1,500 machine is good value; the same repair on a nine-year-old $700 machine is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does a treadmill belt need replacing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically every 5 to 10 years on a maintained machine, and considerably sooner on a neglected one. The part costs $80 to $250 and fitting is $100 to $200 if you do not do it yourself. Belt and deck are usually replaced together because the labour overlaps almost entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does regular maintenance really extend treadmill life?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Substantially. The dominant failure mode is a motor and controller worn out by friction against a dry deck, and lubrication addresses that directly. A maintained mid-range machine reaching ten years against a neglected one failing at five is the usual pattern, for the cost of about an hour a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What treadmill maintenance should I pay a professional for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Anything inside the motor compartment — the motor, the controller board or mains wiring — because of electrical risk and the cost of a misdiagnosis. Lubrication, cleaning, belt tension and tracking are all straightforward DIY. Belt replacement and deck flipping are within reach with basic tools and a second person.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Running Costs</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These four all involve underestimating one cost while overestimating another, and the first is by far the most expensive.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating maintenance as optional to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It costs $20 a year and prevents failures costing $300 to $800. No other decision in treadmill ownership has that payoff ratio. Some manufacturers can also decline a motor warranty claim on a deck that was clearly never lubricated, which turns a skipped hour into a direct financial loss.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a budget machine without checking parts availability</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Routine maintenance costs the same across the range, but repairability does not. On a $500 machine a $250 motor plus labour is close to a total loss, and parts are often unavailable anyway. Mid-range machines are the sweet spot: parts exist, are standardised, and are worth fitting.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting that a subscription dwarfs every other running cost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Ten years of maintenance, one belt-and-deck replacement, electricity and a mat total roughly $560 to $910. A $39 monthly subscription over the same period is $4,680. If you are calculating cost of ownership, the content fee is the number that actually decides it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing a deck without checking whether it flips</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many treadmill decks are reversible — the underside is an unused low-friction surface. Flipping one costs labour only and effectively doubles deck life against a $100 to $300 replacement. Check before ordering a part, because nobody will volunteer this information.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

